**No `period-compare` or `notifications` (AlertStudioPage) route entry.** Navigation is handled by the `RouteHostRegistry`:

**Registry Pattern:** `/Users/atulgupta/Documents/repos/.teslasync-parallel/20260614-231658-97646/slot-21/apps/apple/Sources/App/RouteHostRegistry.swift` (lines 6-22)

```swift
public struct AppRouteHostRegistry {
    private var providers: [AppRoute: () -> AnyView] = [:]

    public mutating func register(_ route: AppRoute, @ViewBuilder _ provider: @escaping () -> some View) {
        providers[route] = { AnyView(provider()) }
    }

    public func view(for route: AppRoute) -> AnyView? {
        providers[route]?()
    }
}
```

**Clarification:** AlertStudioPage is referenced **only in its own Previews.swift**. It is **not wired into the app shell** — the parallel-surface model leaves it standalone, testable, and independently previewable. No central registry entry is required for a new page unless the app's route enum is extended.

**Consequence:** To add `PeriodComparePage` to the live app:
1. Add `case periodCompare` to `AppRoute` enum
2. Extend `RouteHostRegistry.register()` call at app bootstrap (typically in `TeslaSyncApp.swift` or a dependency injection container)
3. Otherwise, the page can live entirely in `feature-views/` and be tested via its own Previews

